 notes="Typed page 14 completes the discussion with 2 nilpotent and starts the exceptional-invariant cases over an algebraically closed field. No annotation by hand is visible apart from the archival numbering. The first displayed equation has a_2 x, without a square; (4.1) on scan 24 has a_2 x². The apparently defective homothétic and sour are retained. In b), the clear references (6.1) and (6.2) seem to refer to (5.1) and (5.2) on scan 25. All are source slips or possible slips, not corrected readings. The b) sentence continues on scan 30.\n\nS029-ETALE: Moving the quadratic equation to the left gives (1+4a_2′)(s²+s)+(a_2′−a_2)=0. When 2 is nilpotent, 1+4a_2′ is a unit, so this is a monic quadratic after division; its derivative is 2s+1, also a unit. This checks the stated degree-two étale step. It does not check the subsequent reduction that makes this equation imply the second equation of (4.3). S029-MOD2: Reducing the formulas (4.4) on scan 24 modulo 2 gives c_4=1 and Δ=a_6, hence 1/j=a_6 modulo 2; this checks the leading term in the written rational expression, not the entire nilpotent argument.",
